WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.940
Are you looking to upgrade your microscope?

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.050
Well microcosmos.store is the place for you!

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:11.340
We’ve got a selection of plan achromatic
objectives ranging from 4x all the way up

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.270
to 100x, and these objectives don’t just
work on The Microcosmos Microscope.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:20.068
They will also work on other DIN-standard
compound microscopes.

00:00:20.068 --> 00:00:25.760
Because of the way lenses are made, regular
achromatic objectives display a curved image

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.253
which results in the edges of the field of
view being slightly distorted and out of focus.

00:00:31.253 --> 00:00:36.950
But these plan objectives produce a flatter
image across the entire field of view, resulting

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:43.149
in an overall sharper image, which is great
if, like us, you enjoy capturing pictures

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:45.480
and videos of your samples.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.930
So go to microcosmos.store if you want to
upgrade your objectives or pick up any other

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:52.130
Microcosmos merchandise.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.180
In 1864, Louis Pasteur stood up in front of
a crowd at the Sorbonne Scientific Soiree

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.775
to deliver an address.

00:01:05.775 --> 00:01:08.161
And he started by saying:

00:01:08.161 --> 00:01:14.943
"A number of imposing problems now have our
best minds in thrall."

00:01:14.943 --> 00:01:20.750
Those imposing questions covered everything
from the history of humankind to the concept

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:27.380
of species, but Pasteur promised the crowd
he would not be answering any of those questions.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:32.021
What Pasteur wanted to address was a question
much more fundamental.

00:01:32.021 --> 00:01:36.070
Can life be created spontaneously?

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:56.119
The concept Pasteur wanted to discuss was
called spontaneous generation, which posited

00:01:56.119 --> 00:02:02.930
that perhaps living things could just like appear
from some other kind of matter, though there

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:06.170
were various ideas about exactly how that
could work.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:11.770
It was an old theory, and it took several
century's worth of experiments to prove that

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:13.417
it was wrong.

00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:18.130
Unfortunately, we of course do not have 
footage of these experiments.

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:24.410
But a year and a half ago, our master of microscopes,
James, was inspired by the idea of spontaneous

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.720
generation and set up his own experiment.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.610
He cut a plastic bottle in half, and flipped
the top half so he could place it in the bottom

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.740
half like a funnel and keep it from spilling.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:40.330
Then he placed his contraption outside to
see what would show up.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.390
The plan was to return a few weeks later,
but…

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:44.494
he forgot.

00:02:44.494 --> 00:02:48.075
And in his defense, he had a lot of other
microbes to watch.

00:02:48.075 --> 00:02:52.830
When James eventually remembered and went
to check on it, he found brownish residue

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.110
and a bunch of dead bugs on the bottom of
the bottle.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:01.030
And of course, because he’s James, the first
thing he did was put it under the microscope

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:06.258
so we could all see the array of microscopic
creatures dwelling within.

00:03:06.258 --> 00:03:12.790
The question that spontaneous generation sought
to answer is how those creatures got into

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.280
that bottle.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.408
And there were several ideas around how life
could spontaneously generate.

00:03:18.408 --> 00:03:24.420
One theory, called abiogenesis, believed that
life could arise from nonliving matter, like

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.500
scallops forming from the sand they’re found
in.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:34.019
Another theory was called heterogenesis, and
it suggested that life could form from another

00:03:34.019 --> 00:03:37.330
species that was living, or that had once
been living—

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:42.080
like insects that show up out of nowhere on
decaying flesh.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.750
Aristotle describes both of these theories
of spontaneous generation in his treatise

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:52.689
The History of Animals, but he was not the
first or only person to believe in spontaneous

00:03:52.689 --> 00:03:53.769
generation.

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:59.163
In Pasteur’s speech, he described an old
recipe to make mice.

00:03:59.724 --> 00:04:06.689
Simply add a dirty shirt to a vessel filled
with wheat grains and then wait 21 days.

00:04:06.689 --> 00:04:12.280
At the end of the waiting period, you will
find the wheat transformed into mice.

00:04:13.066 --> 00:04:15.353
It’s easy enough to laugh at these ideas now.

00:04:15.353 --> 00:04:20.102
Like we know how cells work, and that
they exist, and how they reproduce

00:04:20.102 --> 00:04:25.893
and pass along a life encoded in 
DNA that in turn begets more life.

00:04:25.893 --> 00:04:30.479
And as we watch the creatures from James’
bottle floating across our screen, we know

00:04:30.479 --> 00:04:33.513
enough about microbes to know how they got
there.

00:04:33.513 --> 00:04:38.510
Many of them were in the air, making up an
invisible universe that found its way into

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:43.000
the bottle through rain or wind or some other
element.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.440
But if you lived in a world where you did not
know that microbes can travel through the

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.259
air, James’ experiment would be much harder
to interpret.

00:04:51.259 --> 00:04:58.389
Are there really unseen creatures traveling an 
imperceptible path all around us in the air and the rain

00:04:58.389 --> 00:05:04.337
Or are they the product of a chemical reaction
we just haven’t found the formula to yet?

00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:08.400
On its own, this bottle can’t find the explanation.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.856
In fact, no single experiment could resolve
the spontaneous generation debate.

00:05:13.856 --> 00:05:20.126
Every experiment performed was in conversation
with the experiments and ideas that came before it.

00:05:20.126 --> 00:05:23.891
And each experiment set the scene for the
work that followed.

00:05:23.891 --> 00:05:28.509
For example, in the middle of the 17th century,
an Italian scientist named Francesco Redi

00:05:28.509 --> 00:05:34.250
put meat in sealed jars to demonstrate that
maggots did not spontaneously generate from

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.679
decaying flesh.

00:05:35.679 --> 00:05:40.759
But while his experiment helped people understand
that maggots come from very small eggs, other

00:05:40.759 --> 00:05:43.199
advances complicated matters,

00:05:43.199 --> 00:05:46.857
including our favorite invention, the microscope.

00:05:48.878 --> 00:05:50.919
In Pasteur’s words:

00:05:50.919 --> 00:05:56.434
But at the end of the seventeenth century,
a tremendous discovery, that of the microscope,

00:05:56.434 --> 00:06:03.250
revealed an entire new world to Man, the world
of the infinitesimally small.

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:08.047
So while it had, most assuredly, been vanquished
as far as the higher creatures were concerned,

00:06:08.047 --> 00:06:13.504
the doctrine of spontaneous generation now
reappeared, audaciously declaring:

00:06:13.504 --> 00:06:15.843
Here is my province!

00:06:18.874 --> 00:06:24.630
Microbes are small and often associated with
decaying matter, so they provided a new vessel

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.973
for the ideas of spontaneous generation and
heterogenesis.

00:06:28.973 --> 00:06:34.940
In the middle of the 18th century, one English
biologist named John Needham set out to investigate

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:39.479
the spontaneous generation of microbes by
boiling broth.

00:06:39.479 --> 00:06:44.979
The aim was to kill whatever was living in
there so that after the broth cooled down

00:06:44.979 --> 00:06:50.681
and was then sealed, he could compare it to
another flask of broth that had not been heated.

00:06:50.681 --> 00:06:56.250
When Needham found that both the unheated
and heated broths turned cloudy, he concluded

00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:00.097
that microbes must have spontaneously generated
in those flasks.

00:07:00.097 --> 00:07:02.691
How else could they get there?

00:07:02.691 --> 00:07:06.731
Well we know the answer, 
and we are seeing it in action now:

00:07:06.731 --> 00:07:11.500
there are microbes in the air that found
their way into the experiment.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:15.100
But remember, we did not know 
this at the time.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:20.620
But Lazzaro Spallanzani, an Italian biologist,
had an inkling that this could be the case,

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:25.880
and that perhaps there were microbes in the
air contaminating Needham’s flasks.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:31.379
So Spallanzani repeated the experiment, but
in a way where he could seal the flask before

00:07:31.379 --> 00:07:32.617
boiling it.

00:07:32.617 --> 00:07:37.542
And under Spallanzani’s conditions, no microbes
appeared.

00:07:37.542 --> 00:07:40.229
This seems like a blow for 
spontaneous generation.

00:07:40.229 --> 00:07:45.729
But as scientists better understood the importance
of oxygen to cellular respiration, some began

00:07:45.729 --> 00:07:48.375
to wonder if Spallanzani was wrong.

00:07:48.375 --> 00:07:53.860
What if microbes could spontaneously generate,
just not in the air-deprived environment he

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:55.189
had created?

00:07:59.792 --> 00:08:05.460
Scientists went back and forth over whether
this was the case, engaging in a dialogue

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.629
told through different experimental set-ups.

00:08:08.629 --> 00:08:14.220
But by the time Pasteur took the stage, none
of the experiments before him on either side

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.905
could fully seal the holes in their argument.

00:08:17.905 --> 00:08:23.849
Pasteur disagreed with the theory of spontaneous
generation, and he decided to test his ideas

00:08:23.849 --> 00:08:29.120
by building on the same premise that many
of his predecessors had used in their experiments:

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:34.070
kill off the organisms in a flask and watch
to see if anything grows.

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:38.412
But he needed to prevent contamination from
any organisms potentially flying through the

00:08:38.412 --> 00:08:44.890
air, while also ensuring that there was air
present to nourish organisms if they did manage

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:47.542
to spontaneously generate in the broth.

00:08:47.542 --> 00:08:52.959
This was kind of a complicated and contradictory 
set of parameters for an experiment, and Pasteur

00:08:52.959 --> 00:08:54.980
found an elegant solution.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:00.216
He bent the neck of a flask into an S, creating
a swan-like shape.

00:09:00.216 --> 00:09:04.850
After Pasteur boiled the nutrient broth in
the flask and killed the organisms within

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:09.190
it, the s-shaped neck allowed air to flow
through.

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:14.000
But Pasteur thought the shape would make it
hard for particles traveling in the air to

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.970
get into the broth, trapping microbes before
they could get into the flask.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:26.502
And so it was not a surprise to him that the
broth remained clear, uncontaminated by microbes.

00:09:26.502 --> 00:09:28.959
Pasteur however, was not done.

00:09:28.959 --> 00:09:33.079
He wanted to see if the microbes he expected
to be trapped in the neck of the flask were

00:09:33.079 --> 00:09:34.140
really there.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:40.414
So he tipped the flask to the side and allowed
the broth to reach into the s-shape.

00:09:40.414 --> 00:09:44.190
When he did that, the broth eventually
became cloudy.

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:49.538
He had shown that his broth did not fill with
microbes spontaneously.

00:09:49.538 --> 00:09:54.630
Pasteur gets much of the credit for disproving
spontaneous generation, maybe in part because

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:59.840
it fed into his understanding of fermentation
and the germ theory of disease.

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.408
And when we lay out the story like this, the
path to his discovery seems kind of linear.

00:10:05.408 --> 00:10:09.380
One experiment begets another, which begets
another, and so on—

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:16.160
flask after flask made murky with microbes
like the bottle James set up in his backyard.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:20.850
But there are so many figures missing from
our telling of this story, like John Tyndall,

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:26.920
the Irish physicist who investigated and validated
Pasteur’s theories, or Pasteur’s opposition,

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:30.413
like the pathologist Henry Charlton Bastian.

00:10:30.413 --> 00:10:36.860
And there are so many more characters and
conversations of varied forms and tones that

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:41.873
shaped the way this science was done and the
conclusions that were drawn.

00:10:41.873 --> 00:10:47.149
Because ultimately, the uncovering of 
knowledge is a very human affair—

00:10:47.149 --> 00:10:53.329
a search for beginnings buried in a hidden
world, a treasure hunt with many treasures,

00:10:53.329 --> 00:10:55.042
but no end in sight.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:03.152
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:11:03.152 --> 00:11:05.779
These folks here are our Patreon patrons.

00:11:05.779 --> 00:11:11.725
If you put them in a flask and boil it, I
would be so mad at you.

00:11:11.725 --> 00:11:14.050
How would we make this show without them?

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:16.230
It's not like they spontaneously generate.

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:21.050
They are individual people making individual
decisions because they want things like this

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:23.016
to exist on the Internet.

00:11:23.016 --> 00:11:24.120
Thank you so much to all of them.

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:28.788
And if you would like to see your name here,
you can go to Patreon.com/JourneytoMicro where

00:11:28.788 --> 00:11:32.050
you can also find out about some of the cool
perks you can get!

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:35.090
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James Weiss, you can check out

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:37.269
Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:11:37.269 --> 00:11:41.922
And if you want to see more from us, there's
always a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

